+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright contributors to the vLLM project
+"""sm_80 thin-M BF16 GEMM for the layers a W4A16 GLM-5.x checkpoint leaves BF16.
+
+Ported from Morrowmake/vllm-cmp170hx ``ampere-glm53`` @ 3a2bf16dae
+(``vllm/ampere_thin_gemm/thin_gemm.py`` + ``__init__.py`` + ``warmup.py``).
+The Triton kernel, the measured schedule table and the fallback heuristic are
+copied verbatim; the integration around them is ours (see "Differences from
+MM" below).
+
+    thin_linear(x[M, K] bf16, w[N, K] bf16) -> y[M, N] bf16   == F.linear(x, w)
+
+Why: at M <= 32 a BF16 linear is weight-bandwidth bound. cuBLAS picks few,
+large CTAs for these skinny shapes (or splits K and runs a second reduce
+launch); this kernel splits K across many small CTAs and reduces the FP32
+partials in the last-arriving CTA of each output tile, in a fixed order
+(bitwise run-to-run deterministic, no extra launch). Semantics are exactly
+``F.linear(x, w)`` without bias: BF16 x BF16 products, FP32 accumulation, one
+rounding to BF16. The *summation order* differs from cuBLAS, so results are
+not bitwise equal to cuBLAS; they are equally close to an FP32/FP64 reference.
+
+Default OFF (``VLLM_GLM5_THIN_GEMM=0``): nothing here is imported and
+``dispatch_unquantized_gemm`` returns exactly the callable it returned before.
+
+Differences from MM [deliberate]:
+  * The kernel is reached through a registered custom op
+    (``torch.ops.vllm.glm5_thin_linear``) instead of being called directly, so
+    torch.compile'd modules (the DFlash drafter is ``@support_torch_compile``)
+    see one opaque op and the row-count branch is taken at run time / capture
+    time, never baked into a Dynamo guard.
+  * Workspace (split-K partials and arrival counters) is keyed per (N, K, M,
+    SPLIT_K) and per device instead of per byte size with explicit "lanes".
+    Two thin GEMMs only share a buffer if they have the same shape, so a
+    shared-expert GEMM on the MoE aux stream can never race a different-shape
+    GEMM on the main stream. (Same shape on two streams at once does not occur
+    in our decode graphs.)
+  * Inside a CUDA-graph capture, a (M, N, K) that was never run eagerly (so
+    its Triton binary or workspace would be created inside the capture) falls
+    back to ``F.linear`` and logs once, instead of compiling/allocating in the
+    capture. The warmup below normally makes this unreachable.
+"""
